# netsurf: resolve fetch() at the header block, not the first body byte
#
# North-star B1 follow-up (doc 13 section 5, item 3). Until now the first
# progress tick -- and with it fetch()'s promise resolution and XHR's
# HEADERS_RECEIVED -- waited for the first body byte: only FETCH_DATA
# scheduled xhr_progress. A server that sends its headers and then waits
# (an SSE endpoint holding the stream, a slow-start API) left the fetch()
# promise pending for no reason: the Response (status + headers) is fully
# determined at the end of the header block.
#
# The fetchers deliver the header block line by line as FETCH_HEADER and
# the block ends with the empty line. When it arrives, the bridge now
# schedules the same progress tick FETCH_DATA would: it sets
# status/headers on the JS object and calls __onNativeProgress, so
# fetch() resolves and the body then streams in through the existing
# __xhrNativeTakeBytes pump. No JS-side change: the polyfill already
# resolves on the first progress tick.
#
# fetch_http_code() is not set until the first body byte (or completion),
# so the status is parsed from the accumulated status line (last one wins:
# 1xx interim blocks and redirect hops each contribute one) and stored in
# s->status for the deferred tick, which only overwrites it when
# fetch_http_code() is > 0.
#
# Two cases do not tick early: 1xx interim responses (more header blocks
# follow) and 3xx with a Location header (the fetcher follows the redirect
# itself; ticking now would resolve fetch() with the 3xx instead of the
# final response, whose block ticks). One early tick per response; the
# redirect path resets the flag. Non-HTTP fetchers (no status line, no
# empty terminator) are untouched.
#
# Test: testpages/pages/JS-fetch-headers.html + the /slow-headers endpoint
# in tools/vita-sim/httpd.py (headers, flush, 2 s wait, then the body),
# gated in tools/run-vita-sim.sh over HTTP and HTTPS.
diff --git a/content/handlers/javascript/quickjs/macos9_js_fetch.c b/content/handlers/javascript/quickjs/macos9_js_fetch.c
